  "worked_solution": "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+9)! & =(n+9)(n+8)(n+7)! \\\\ (n-8)! & =(n-8)(n-9)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+7)!$ and $(n-9)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!} & =\\frac{[(n-8)\\cdot (n-9)!]\\cdot [(n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+7)!]}{(n-9)!\\cdot (n+7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot (n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}}{\\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}} \\\\  & =(n-8)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+9) \\\\  & =(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
